// ═══ wait() — releases lock, waits for notify ═══
synchronized (lock) {
    while (!condition) {
        lock.wait();        // RELEASES lock! Other threads CAN enter!
                            // State: WAITING
                            // Resumes: when notify()/notifyAll() called
    }
    // lock re-acquired here
}
// MUST be inside synchronized — else IllegalMonitorStateException
// MUST use while loop (spurious wake-ups)
// Used for: producer-consumer, condition waiting

// ═══ sleep() — does NOT release lock, timed pause ═══
Thread.sleep(5000);         // Pauses current thread for 5 seconds
                            // State: TIMED_WAITING
                            // Does NOT release ANY lock!
                            // Resumes: after 5 seconds OR interrupted

synchronized (lock) {
    Thread.sleep(5000);     // Lock is HELD for 5 seconds!
    // Other threads trying to enter synchronized → BLOCKED for 5 seconds!
}
// Can be called ANYWHERE — no synchronized requirement
// Static method: Thread.sleep() affects CURRENT thread

// ═══ yield() — hint to scheduler, no guarantees ═══
Thread.yield();             // "I'm willing to give up my CPU timeslice"
                            // State: stays RUNNABLE
                            // Scheduler may IGNORE this hint!
                            // No lock behavior — irrelevant to locks
// Rarely used in production. No guarantees. Platform-dependent.
// Used in: spin-wait loops, testing (force context switches)

// ═══ COMPARISON TABLE ═══
// Feature         wait()               sleep()              yield()
// Releases lock?  YES!                 NO!                  N/A (no lock)
// State           WAITING              TIMED_WAITING        RUNNABLE
// Resumes when    notify()/notifyAll() timeout/interrupt    scheduler decides
// Requires        synchronized block   nothing              nothing
// Purpose         inter-thread comms   timed delay          scheduler hint
// Throws          InterruptedException InterruptedException nothing
// Static?         NO (instance method) YES (Thread.sleep)   YES (Thread.yield)
